<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rubén Darío</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Félix Rubén García Sarmiento</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rubén Darío was a Nicaraguan poet, journalist and diplomat, the founder and leading figure of modernismo in Spanish-language literature. His collections Azul (1888), Prosas profanas (1896) and Cantos de vida y esperanza (1905) renewed Spanish verse with new metres and imagery drawn from French poetry. Living in Chile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he became the most influential Hispanic poet of his age, and he died in León, Nicaragua.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ruben-dario.jpg" alt="Rubén Darío" width="291" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rub%C3%A9n_Dar%C3%ADo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
